  <h2 id="quick">Quick Reference — Per-Project Rates (2026)</h2>
  <div class="callout">
    <b>Quick Take:</b><br>
    • 🪙 <b>Basic social media edit (Reels/Shorts):</b> ₹1,000 – ₹5,000 per video<br>
    • ⚡ <b>Standard YouTube video (cuts + light color + subtitles):</b> ₹3,000 – ₹10,000<br>
    • 🏆 <b>Advanced (motion graphics, heavy grading, VFX):</b> ₹5,000 – ₹25,000+<br>
    • 🎬 <b>Corporate / brand film:</b> ₹5,000 – ₹15,000 per video<br>
    • 📽️ <b>Documentary / long-form / event:</b> ₹15,000 – ₹50,000+<br>
    • 🔁 <b>Monthly retainer (10-30 videos):</b> ₹25,000 – ₹1,50,000+
  </div>

  <h2 id="experience">Experience Ke Hisaab Se Pricing</h2>
  <p>Rate sirf skill pe nahi, portfolio aur trust pe bhi depend karta hai. Ek naya editor jisne 5 videos edit ki hain, wahi rate nahi maang sakta jo 5 saal se kaam kar raha editor maangta hai — bhale hi dono ki technical skill same ho. Client trust ek asset hai jo time ke saath banta hai.</p>

  <div class="brand-card">
    <h4>Beginner (0–1 saal experience)</h4>
    <p>Is stage pe goal paisa kamana nahi, portfolio banana hai. Simple cuts, trims, basic transitions, captions — is level ka kaam. Zyada charge maangne se pehle 5-10 solid portfolio pieces chahiye, chahe unmein se kuch free ya bahut kam rate pe kiye ho.</p>
    <span class="spec-chip">₹500 – ₹2,000 per video</span><span class="spec-chip">₹15,000 – ₹30,000/month agar full-time</span><span class="spec-chip">Goal: Portfolio, not income</span>
  </div>

  <div class="brand-card">
    <h4>Intermediate (1–3 saal experience)</h4>
    <p>Yahan aap multi-layer timelines, basic color grading, sound design, aur consistent turnaround handle kar sakte ho. Clients repeat aane lagte hain — yeh sign hai ki rate badhane ka time aa gaya hai.</p>
    <span class="spec-chip">₹3,000 – ₹10,000 per video</span><span class="spec-chip">₹30,000 – ₹60,000/month</span><span class="spec-chip">Retainer clients possible</span>
  </div>

  <div class="brand-card">
    <h4>Advanced / Senior (3+ saal, specialized skill)</h4>
    <p>Node-based color grading, motion graphics, VFX compositing, ya large-channel management jaise specialized skills is stage pe rate ko significantly upar le jaate hain. Yahan aap project ke complexity aur outcome (views, conversions) ke hisaab se price karte ho, sirf "time spent" ke hisaab se nahi.</p>
    <span class="spec-chip">₹8,000 – ₹25,000+ per video</span><span class="spec-chip">₹60,000 – ₹1,50,000+/month</span><span class="spec-chip">Retainers ₹50,000+ common</span>
  </div>

  <h2 id="per-minute">Per-Minute vs Per-Project vs Retainer — Kaunsa Model Chunein</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Pricing Model</th><th>Best For</th><th>Risk</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Per-Minute</strong></td><td>Long-form content jahan length predictable hai (podcasts, interviews)</td><td>Complex short clips underpriced ho sakte hain</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Per-Project (Flat Fee)</strong></td><td>Clear-scope deliverables jaise ek YouTube video ya ek Reel</td><td>Scope creep — revisions unlimited maang li toh loss</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Per-Hour</strong></td><td>Ongoing work jaha scope week-to-week vary karta hai</td><td>Client ko lagta hai aap "slow" kaam kar rahe ho</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Monthly Retainer</strong></td><td>Regular clients jinhe 10-30 videos/month chahiye</td><td>Workload spike ho sakta hai bina extra pay ke agar scope clear nahi</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="callout"><b>Sabse safe practice:</b> Per-project flat fee quote karte waqt hamesha revision limit likh do — "2 rounds of revisions included" jaisa kuch. Unlimited revisions ek flat fee pe dena sabse common tareeka hai jisse editors apna kaam underprice kar dete hain.</div>

  <h2 id="salary">Full-Time Salary vs Freelance Income</h2>
  <p>Full-time job aur freelancing dono ke apne trade-offs hain. Job mein stability milti hai, freelancing mein ceiling zyada hai but income unpredictable rehta hai shuru mein.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Level</th><th>Full-Time Monthly Salary</th><th>Annual (Full-Time)</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Fresher</strong></td><td>₹15,000 – ₹30,000</td><td>₹2 – ₹4 lakh</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mid-Level (2-4 yrs)</strong></td><td>₹30,000 – ₹60,000</td><td>₹3.5 – ₹8 lakh</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Senior / Lead Editor</strong></td><td>₹60,000 – ₹1,25,000+</td><td>₹8 – ₹15+ lakh</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="chart-note">Numbers metro cities (Mumbai, Bangalore, Delhi) aur established production houses/agencies ke industry benchmarks par based hain — Tier-2/3 cities aur solo YouTubers ke liye range niche shift ho sakta hai.</p>

  <h2 id="factors">Rate Decide Karne Waale Real Factors</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Turnaround time:</strong> Rush jobs (24-48 hours) pe 30-50% premium charge karna normal hai — yeh aapka rest/other-clients ka time hai jo squeeze ho raha hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Complexity:</strong> Sirf cuts vs motion graphics vs full color grading vs VFX — har layer rate upar leke jaata hai. Color grading aur motion graphics skills 40-80% tak premium justify karte hain cut-only editing ke comparison mein.</li>
    <li><strong>Client ka budget aur industry:</strong> Corporate/brand clients generally creator/individual clients se zyada pay kar sakte hain — but unki expectations aur revision cycles bhi zyada structured hoti hain.</li>
    <li><strong>Aapka portfolio aur proof of work:</strong> Ek strong showreel ya visible results (channel growth, engagement) rate negotiation mein sabse bada leverage hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Recurring vs one-off:</strong> Retainer clients ko thoda discount dena reasonable hai kyunki unse predictable, steady income milta hai — but hamesha ek minimum floor rate rakho jisse neeche na jao.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="niche">Niche Skills Jo Rate Ko Significantly Badhate Hain</h2>
  <p>Har editor "editing" nahi bech raha — asal mein aap ek specific outcome bech rahe ho: attention, conversions, ya brand perception. Jitni specialized skill, utna kam competition, utna zyada pricing power. Yahan wahi skills hain jo actually market mein rate ko upar le jaate hain:</p>

  <div class="brand-card">
    <h4>Node-Based Color Grading</h4>
    <p>Sirf "colors thik karna" nahi — proper node tree, secondary qualifiers, Power Windows, aur skin-tone matching jaisi skills bahut kam editors ke paas hoti hain. Yeh skill akela ek editor ko generalist se specialist bana deta hai, aur clients specialist ko premium dete hain kyunki unhe alag se colorist hire nahi karna padta.</p>
    <span class="spec-chip">+30-60% rate premium</span>
  </div>

  <div class="brand-card">
    <h4>Motion Graphics &amp; Fusion/After Effects</h4>
    <p>Kinetic typography, animated lower-thirds, custom transitions — yeh skills brand videos aur corporate content mein bahut demand mein hain. Cut-only editor se motion graphics editor 40-80% zyada charge kar sakta hai same project pe.</p>
    <span class="spec-chip">+40-80% rate premium</span>
  </div>

  <div class="brand-card">
    <h4>Sound Design &amp; Fairlight Mixing</h4>
    <p>Zyada editors audio ko afterthought treat karte hain — sirf background music daal dete hain. Jo editor proper ducking, EQ, aur clean dialogue mix de sakta hai, woh un clients ko attract karta hai jo audio quality ki value samajhte hain (podcasts, interviews, documentary).</p>
    <span class="spec-chip">+20-40% rate premium</span>
  </div>

  <h2 id="negotiate">Client Se Rate Negotiate Kaise Karein</h2>
  <p>Negotiation mein sabse bada mistake hai apna rate directly bata dena bina client ka budget ya scope samjhe. Behtar approach yeh hai — pehle client se poochho unka deliverable kya hai, kitni videos, kitna turnaround, kya revisions included honge. Uske baad tiered pricing offer karo: ek basic package (cuts + captions), ek enhanced package (+ motion graphics), aur ek premium package (+ color grading + sound design). Zyadatar clients middle tier choose karte hain — yeh psychology hai, sirf ek price dene se zyada effective hota hai.</p>
  <p>Agar client aapke rate pe bargain kare, discount dene ke bajaye scope kam karo — "Is budget mein main 2 revisions include kar sakta hoon instead of unlimited" jaisa kuch. Isse aapka floor rate protect rehta hai, aur client ko bhi lagta hai ki unhone kuch negotiate kiya.</p>
  <div class="callout"><b>International clients ka angle:</b> Agar aap Upwork, Fiverr, ya direct international clients ke saath kaam kar rahe ho, USD/GBP rates charge karo jo INR mein convert hone ke baad bhi Indian domestic rate se kaafi zyada baithte hain. Payment ke liye Wise ya Payoneer jaise platforms use karo — direct USD wire transfers mein TDS aur RBI compliance complications aa sakte hain.</div>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Beginners Jo Sabse Common Galtiyan Karte Hain</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bahut kam charge karna "kaam mil jaaye" ke chakkar mein</strong> — isse client base hi low-budget clients ka ban jaata hai, aur rate badhana mushkil ho jaata hai baad mein.</li>
    <li><strong>Revision limit define na karna</strong> — "jab tak client khush na ho" jaisi flat-fee deal loss mein badal sakti hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Apna time track na karna</strong> — agar aapko pata hi nahi ki ek video mein aapke kitne ghante lag rahe hain, aap pricing ko sahi justify nahi kar sakte.</li>
    <li><strong>Sirf "time spent" pe price karna, "value delivered" pe nahi</strong> — ek video jo client ko lakhon ki sales laayegi, uska price sirf editing hours se decide nahi hona chahiye.</li>
    <li><strong>Rate kabhi na badhaana</strong> — jaise-jaise experience aur demand badhti hai, purane clients ke liye bhi rate periodically review karna zaroori hai.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="invoicing">Invoicing, Contracts Aur GST Basics — Jo Koi Nahi Sikhata</h2>
  <p>Editing skill sikhna ek cheez hai, business chalana bilkul alag skill hai. Yahan basics hain jo har freelance editor ko pata hone chahiye India mein kaam karte waqt:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Advance lena:</strong> Naye client se hamesha 30-50% advance lo project start karne se pehle. Isse aapka time protect hota hai agar client ghost kar de beech mein.</li>
    <li><strong>Simple contract/agreement:</strong> Ek WhatsApp message ya email mein bhi scope, deliverables, revision limit, aur payment terms likh do — legal document na sahi, lekin ek written record dono paksho ko accountable rakhta hai.</li>
    <li><strong>Income tax:</strong> Agar aapki annual income ₹2.5 lakh se zyada hai, aap income tax ke liye liable ho. Freelancers "Profits and Gains from Business or Profession" ke under file karte hain — presumptive taxation scheme (Section 44ADA) chhote freelancers ke liye simple option hai.</li>
    <li><strong>GST registration:</strong> Zaroori hai sirf tab jab annual turnover ₹20 lakh cross kare (₹10 lakh special category states mein). Zyadatar beginner-to-mid level editors ko iski zaroorat nahi padti shuru mein.</li>
    <li><strong>International payments:</strong> Agar international clients se kaam mil raha hai, Wise ya Payoneer jaise platforms use karo — direct USD wire transfer mein TDS deduction aur RBI compliance complications aa sakti hain jo chhote freelancers ke liye unnecessary hassle create karti hain.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout"><b>Reminder:</b> Yeh general guidance hai, financial/legal advice nahi. Apni specific situation ke liye ek CA (Chartered Accountant) se consult karna sabse safe approach hai, especially jaise-jaise income badhti hai.</div>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQs</h2>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Beginner video editor ko India mein kitna charge karna chahiye?</h3>
    <p>Simple social media edits ke liye ₹500-₹2,000 per video se start karna reasonable hai, jab tak aapke paas strong portfolio nahi hai. Priority paisa nahi, portfolio building honi chahiye is stage pe.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Per-hour ya per-project — kaunsa better hai?</h3>
    <p>Clear-scope, defined-deliverable kaam (ek YouTube video, ek Reel) ke liye per-project better hai. Ongoing, scope-varying kaam (jaise channel management) ke liye per-hour ya monthly retainer better fit hai.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Freelancing better hai ya full-time job?</h3>
    <p>Depends aapki risk tolerance pe. Job stability deti hai but ceiling limited hoti hai. Freelancing mein income unpredictable ho sakta hai shuru mein, but experienced freelance editors ka income potential full-time salary se kaafi upar ja sakta hai, khaas kar international clients ke saath.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Kya GST registration zaroori hai freelance editor ke liye?</h3>
    <p>Sirf tab jab aapka annual turnover ₹20 lakh se zyada ho jaaye (₹10 lakh special category states mein). Zyadatar beginner aur mid-level freelance editors ko yeh threshold cross karne mein time lagta hai, isliye shuru mein GST registration ki zaroorat nahi hoti — lekin income badhne ke saath ek CA se timeline confirm karwana sahi rahega.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Color grading skill se kitna extra charge kar sakte hain?</h3>
    <p>Node-based color grading aur motion graphics jaise specialized skills typically 40-80% premium justify karte hain basic cut-only editing ke comparison mein — kyunki yeh skills seekhne mein zyada time aur practice lagta hai.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>Kya monthly retainer per-project pricing se better hai?</h3>
    <p>Retainers steady, predictable income dete hain aur client relationship ko long-term banate hain — ek baar trust ban jaaye toh negotiation baar-baar nahi karni padti. Lekin scope clearly define karna zaroori hai (kitni videos, kitni revisions per video) taaki workload unexpectedly badh na jaaye bina extra pay ke.</p>
  </div>

